WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.517 --> 00:00:02.970
(upbeat music)
(applause)

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.853
- Good morning.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.080
So let's start with this.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.960
How many people in this
room by show of hands

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.960
have no idea who I am,
raise your hand, that hurts.

00:00:16.288 --> 00:00:18.650
(laughing)

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.040
I thought that would be the case.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.150
First of all, I've really been enjoying.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.860
I'm a business man, I'm an entrepreneur.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.540
Obviously I make content, I write books.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.373
But I'm extremely proud
that for the last 20 years

00:00:30.373 --> 00:00:33.550
from literally the day that
I left college till this

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.927
moment right now, I've
been operating a business.

00:00:36.927 --> 00:00:41.927
And spending the last week or
two looking at this industry

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.350
and the dynamics was
interesting to me 'cause I think

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.530
context really matters and
I think one of the things

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.910
that affords me the ability
to continue to speak at

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.390
conferences, which I enjoy so
much so thank you for having

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.190
me is that I don't have a can speech.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.250
I have things I believe in
but I think context matters.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:04.250
And I was exciting digging
into this world because one,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.210
as I was digging I realized a
couple of my friends were in

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.650
it so I was able to get under
the hood a little bit more

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:13.660
and two, this is a perfect cliche example

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.600
of an industry and I know
there's multiple parties

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:20.400
and chain in this room and
I'll try to address both

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.100
or all three where I have
empathy to why the majority

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.540
of people don't think or
fully understand what the

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.238
impact of a Facebook or
an Instagram or a Snapchat

00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:34.060
mean to them in a business world.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.210
And as you can imagine for
me, that's super exciting

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.270
'cause that means literally
all I have to do is over

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:46.270
the course of the next hour
articulate in business terms

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.680
and create the emotion to
the action of literally

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.600
just one or two individuals in this

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:54.000
entire conference to
actually make an impact.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.050
I think what's most fascinating
for me is industries,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.620
look if you sell sneakers
to 20 year olds, I think

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.310
it makes sense that
Instagram or Facebook matter.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.700
When you're in a b to b
dynamic and trying to win big

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.810
accounts or trying to
become a supplier to a big

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.870
organization, it becomes less obvious.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.470
However it's funny I
get to see Mickey Cloud

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.889
here who runs our Chattanooga office.

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:22.180
When I looked at him, I'm
thinking back to the greatest

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.610
successes at Vayner
Media the last six years

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.360
and when I think about our
biggest financial impact

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.130
it has been far more
than General Electrics

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.480
of the SAPs and the B to b
business than it has been

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.440
the Budweiser's or the Pepsi's
because as a lot of you

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.050
know when you play b to b and
I'm in the b to b business

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.730
now, all it takes is one
account for it to start

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.450
getting really interesting
and so because so many of

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.930
you don't know who I am
I'm gonna give you a little

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.190
bit of my origin story because
it's gonna give you a lot

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.600
of ammo to actually make the

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.210
leap that I hope that you consider.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.210
I was born in Belarus in
the former Soviet Union.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.700
I came to the US when I was three.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.340
We lived a very humble
beginning as you can imagine.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.870
The economy wasn't super great.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.120
We didn't speak English.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.640
We have 100 bucks, I
lived with seven family

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.900
members in a studio apartment in Queens.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.180
So it was super humble
and obviously my parents

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.400
are very much my heroes one because my mom

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.740
is the greatest parent of all time.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.010
And two because my dad works his face off

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.520
and even though I slept
under the same roof literally

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.630
every day of my life with
my dad in the first 14 years

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.500
of my life, I literally
never saw him because

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.120
he left at seven am or six
am and he came home at 10 pm.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.340
And he worked seven days a week.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.720
How many people here are
immigrants or children

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.810
of immigrants, raise your hands, awesome.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.310
So this group that just raised you hands,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.570
they know that immigrants
in America have absolutely

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.300
figured out one of the core
secrets which is work your

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.553
face off and don't buy dumb shit.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.998
And so that's what my family
did for seven or eight years.

00:04:07.998 --> 00:04:11.020
My dad wasn't making that
much money but a funny thing

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.860
happens when you buy nothing
but food at the lowest

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.250
possible price and save,
save, save and very quickly,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.560
only six years after coming
to America with 100 bucks,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.130
my dad was able to buy a small
liquor store in New Jersey.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.110
We moved to New Jersey, that
is where my entrepreneurial

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.010
career began, when I was six I had a five

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.430
lemonade stand franchise.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.840
I manipulated my friends
into standing behind

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.280
the lemonade all day and
that is when I became

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.910
obsessed with attention and
this is really interesting.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.300
As you can see up here,
I day trade attention,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.230
culture and build businesses.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:52.230
At six years old, I would
spend my summer days literally

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.190
walking up and down the
streets of New Jersey

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:59.110
sitting down on the curb
watching cars drive by

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:03.030
so that I could figure
out which tree or post

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.010
was better for me to put
a sign that said Lemonade,

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:10.130
a quarter, that is DNA,
that's like somebody singing.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.140
That's like somebody throwing a pitch.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.650
That is just pure DNA, I
didn't read a Seth Godin

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:17.740
book or go to Harvard
Business School to figure out

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.730
that attention was the number one asset.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.720
The one thing that
combines every person here

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.510
from the camera man back
there who are producing

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.950
to the industry that is in
this room from me to Mickey

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.560
to anybody else that sneaked
in, the one thing that

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.410
fundamentally connects all
of us is before you tell

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.850
me how great your product and service is,

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.910
you need my attention so that I listen.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.120
I am obsessed with attention.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.630
I am obsessed with this device
because it has the attention.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.390
I am unemotional if it's ruining the kids

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.460
and they don't know how to
engage or they you were sad

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.930
that people go out to dinner
and don't talk to each other.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:57.580
Technology does not care
about your feelings.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.455
And so I as a human may
have some opinions about

00:06:00.455 --> 00:06:05.455
parenthood and society
and privacy as a human.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:10.130
But as a business man, I am
unemotional and not romantic.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.260
All I do is follow where the
attention is and I figure

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.120
out how to story tell in there
to create the action that

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.960
I desire whether that is
to sell a pair of sneakers

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.240
or a book or my agency or
whether that's to raise money

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.010
for the Crohn's and
Colitis Foundation 'cause

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:28.670
my brother has it or get
my friend elected mayor.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:32.540
It is completely arbitrary
but there is one religion.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:35.670
Where is the attention of
our consumer and I think the

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.280
thing, the delta, the most
important and exciting thing

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.230
for me in this room today is
yes there is some attention,

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.790
of course, at a conference,
if you set up a booth.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:50.580
And yes there is attention
if you spam everybody

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.180
24 hours a day on LinkedIn
or cold call or decide

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:58.800
to buy a full page ad in the
business to business magazine

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.700
that four people open, yes,
there is some attention.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:08.090
But in 2018 if you do not
realize that that is overpriced

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.970
attention, then you are vulnerable

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:13.225
for the growth of your business.

00:07:13.225 --> 00:07:15.010
I spend all my time
thinking about overpriced

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.430
and underpriced attention and
what's happening in culture.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:23.040
I navigated from my lemonade
stand to baseball cards

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.850
because I'm 42 and in
1987, eight, nine, 1990,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.360
baseball cards was the culture.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.530
Every kid was buying them
and that was my life.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:36.950
I was really unusual eastern
European immigrant that

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.990
came during that time,
every one of my friends,

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.250
every one of my parent's
friend's kids were straight a

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.200
students 'cause education was the way out.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.670
If you go to Instagram literally right now

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.580
and look at my account
Garyvee two posts ago,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.650
I posted my high school report card 'cause

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:57.650
nobody believes me but I was
a straight d and f student.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:01.810
Somewhere around fourth
grade I realized that Saturn

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:06.300
was not going to impact my life
and I didn't care about it.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.300
And so self awareness is a son of a bitch.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:16.130
And honestly besides health
for everybody in this room,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.390
I wish you self awareness
'cause once you understand

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.650
who you are, you can do a lot of damage.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.120
All of us suck at a lot of stuff.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.800
All of us are good at a lot of stuff.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.310
The game is to figure that out.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.440
I, through good parenting,
through circumstance,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.720
through who knows really
what got very self aware

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.518
very early in my life and
listen, at 13, listen,

00:08:35.518 --> 00:08:40.020
it was tough for my friend's
parents to say I was a loser

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:41.050
'cause I got d's and f's.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:43.290
It wasn't fun for my
teachers to say that I wasn't

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:44.700
gonna amount to anything.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.400
Here was my biggest problem.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.980
I was 13 years old, I was
making $3,000 a weekend

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.730
selling baseball cards, I had
$47,000 in cash under my bed

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.092
and I wasn't selling weed,
I thought I was amazing.

00:08:58.092 --> 00:09:02.592
(laughing)
(applause)

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.170
I turned 14 and then
my dad ruined my life.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:13.090
I'm the oldest son, first
generation, born in the old

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.270
country, that means one
thing, 14th birthday,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.220
all weekends and summer
vacations are eliminated.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.230
I got work in the liquor
store, 12 hours a day,

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.600
bagging ice, two bucks
an hour, I go from making

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.600
$3,000 a weekend, being a
king, to making $24 a weekend.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.880
My dad was born in the Soviet
Union which means that he

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.253
hates his employees.
(laughing)

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.080
Which meant the four foot 11
me going into that environment

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.740
was really bad for me
'cause they hated him back.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:48.330
And so those two years
literally every weekend,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:52.250
every summer vacation, my
leisure, my childhood ended in

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:55.691
eighth grade, I had my friends
but I just never saw them

00:09:55.691 --> 00:09:59.270
outside of school so I
was old school, right?

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:02.960
I was much more a merchant's
son from the 50s, 40s and 60s.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.460
Just happened to be growing
up in the 80s and 90s.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:08.570
And so for two years I
was stuck in a basement.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:12.880
I call those the dark years
and then finally my junior

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.840
year of high school when
I turned 17 I was allowed

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:19.840
to come upstairs and then my
life really took a big change.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.090
I realized that people collected wine.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.330
And because I was so into
collecting baseball cards

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:27.970
and those things, that became
the passion connector for me

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.430
and basically I went from
reading the Beckett Baseball

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.510
Card Guide in school 24
hours a day to reading

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:36.877
the Wine Spectator in
school 24 hours a day.

00:10:36.877 --> 00:10:41.010
And 17, 18, I decided, first
of all it was fun to get

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.800
to know my dad even though
he was rough, it was fun.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.970
And two, it was the family business,

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:49.880
decided I was gonna go
into it and in 1993, 1994,

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.450
I thought I would open up,
I used to have it in my mind

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.280
as I'll open up 1800 of these.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.570
I will build the Toys 'R Us of wine.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:01.800
I will sell the franchise one
day, buy the New York Jets,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.800
win some Super Bowls, call it a day.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.719
So that was basically the
plan at 17 and basically

00:11:07.719 --> 00:11:10.610
the Jets part is still in play.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.730
Everything else has evolved.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:16.560
So I was a terrible student.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.430
I thought I wasn't gonna go to college.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.220
Another thing some of the kids
of immigrants in here know

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.470
is that your parents don't
know stuff so my mom came to

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:28.480
me February of my senior
year, literally 90 days before

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.470
we graduate and says to me
so what are you doin' about

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.960
college and I laughed and I
said mom, what do you talking

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.100
about, we missed it,
I didn't take my SATs.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:41.603
I didn't apply for school,
I have to go to work in the

00:11:41.603 --> 00:11:44.701
business, I knew my mom would
be embarrassed if I went

00:11:44.701 --> 00:11:49.010
to a community college so I
knew that was her Kryptonite

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:52.440
so I was super pumped, I
thought I figured it all out.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.390
Some of you may have a Soviet mother.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:56.460
She looked me dead in
the face and she said

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.040
you better figure it
out or I'm gonna punch

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.138
you in the face until you bleed.

00:12:00.138 --> 00:12:04.350
So as fate would have it,
this is literally how I went

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:05.970
to college, I think this
will be interesting to you.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:10.020
Literally the next day I
get a postcard in the mail,

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:13.060
in the mail a postcard, we
got mail, I went to go get

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.830
the mail, there's a post
card from Mount Ida College.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.930
Literally I filled out
my name and my address.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:22.000
I sent it in and that's
how I went to college.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.970
So this is the college I
went to just so everybody's

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.230
grounded, I'm in my
dorm room Freshman year.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:32.210
I'm playing Madden '94
dominating by the way and my

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.400
friend runs in and goes you
have to come and see this.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:37.800
I finish off my game, I
go in and I see seven guys

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.870
standing around a computer, this is 1994.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.230
I had been on a computer for
maybe 15 hours in my life

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:46.070
because I failed typing
class and computers

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.810
so I never did it, I had a
Word Processor that I was

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.423
supposed to use, I never did.

00:12:51.619 --> 00:12:52.452
And I'm looking at this computer

00:12:52.452 --> 00:12:54.145
and it was the first time I heard.

00:12:54.145 --> 00:12:54.978
(mimicking connectivity signals)

00:12:54.978 --> 00:12:59.220
That whole thing and
looking around there's not

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.650
that many youngsters,
how many people here do

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.640
not remember the world pre
internet, raise your hand,

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.880
you're that young, not
that you have a bad memory.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:11.000
You're just under 30, raise
your hands, okay good, not many.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:15.080
So for everybody else we
actually remember the world

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.700
pre internet which is crazy
if you think about it now.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:23.700
But we lived it and so the
first time I saw the internet

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:27.170
I literally sat there for
four hours watching other

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.560
people go on it 'cause it was so crazy.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.040
A couple of other things
that were so crazy for the

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.750
three youngsters, it was
$2.99 an hour from AOL

